    {
      "id": "s3",
      "kind": "content",
      "heading": "How the plant builds them",
      "narration": "Inside the plant, cannabinoids are made in tiny glandular structures called trichomes, mainly on the flowers. The biosynthesis starts with a molecule called cannabigerolic acid, CBGA. Enzymes then convert CBGA into different acidic cannabinoids: THCA, CBDA, or CBCA, depending on which enzyme the plant expresses. These acidic forms are what the living plant actually contains. When the plant material is heated or stored for a long time, the acids lose a carbon dioxide group, a process called decarboxylation, and become the neutral forms: THC, CBD and CBC.",
      "bullets": [
        {
          "text": "Made in glandular trichomes, mostly on the flowers",
          "at": 0.08
        },
        {
          "text": "Starting point: cannabigerolic acid (CBGA)",
          "at": 0.25
        },
        {
          "text": "Plant enzymes convert CBGA into THCA, CBDA or CBCA",
          "at": 0.4
        },
        {
          "text": "Heat or ageing removes CO₂ (decarboxylation) → THC, CBD, CBC",
          "at": 0.7
        }
      ],
