    {
      "id": "s5",
      "kind": "content",
      "heading": "Shape and handedness",
      "narration": "Two properties of these molecules shaped the whole history of the field. First, they are highly lipophilic: they dissolve in fats and oils but hardly at all in water. This is why they accumulate in cell membranes, and it is also why early scientists assumed they might act on membranes in a non-specific way. Second, THC is chiral: it exists as mirror-image forms, like a left and a right hand. The natural form is (−)-trans-Δ9-THC. Its mirror image is far less active. That difference was an early clue that the molecule must fit into a specific, shaped binding site, a receptor.",
      "bullets": [
        {
          "text": "Highly lipophilic: soluble in fat and oil, barely in water",
          "at": 0.08
        },
        {
          "text": "Accumulates in cell membranes",
          "at": 0.3
        },
        {
          "text": "THC is chiral: mirror-image forms, like left and right hands",
          "at": 0.5
        },
        {
          "text": "The mirror image is far less active → evidence for a shaped binding site",
          "at": 0.78
        }
      ],
      "visual": {
        "type": "compare",
        "left": {
          "title": "(−)-trans-Δ9-THC",
          "rows": [
            "The natural form",
            "Fits the receptor",
            "Active at nanomolar concentrations"
          ]
        },
        "right": {
          "title": "(+)-trans-Δ9-THC",
          "rows": [
            "The mirror image",
            "Poor fit",
            "Far less active"
          ]
        },
        "at": 0.5
      },
