    {
      "id": "s6",
      "kind": "content",
      "heading": "Three families of cannabinoids",
      "narration": "Today the term cannabinoid covers three families. Phytocannabinoids are the plant molecules we have just met. Endocannabinoids are molecules made by the body itself, which we will meet in episode four; they look chemically quite different, but they act on the same receptors. Synthetic cannabinoids are compounds designed in the laboratory. Some of them, such as CP-55,940, WIN 55,212-2, and HU-210, were made as research tools, and they turned out to be essential for discovering the receptors, because they could be made radioactive and bind much more tightly than THC.",
      "bullets": [
        {
          "text": "Phytocannabinoids: made by the plant (THC, CBD, CBG…)",
          "at": 0.1
        },
        {
          "text": "Endocannabinoids: made by the body (anandamide, 2-AG)",
          "at": 0.3
        },
        {
          "text": "Synthetic cannabinoids: designed in the laboratory",
          "at": 0.55
        },
        {
          "text": "Research tools such as CP-55,940 and HU-210 made receptor discovery possible",
          "at": 0.75
        }
      ],
